<%- include('partials/head') %>

<div class="page-head">
  <h1>RaidProtector Privacy Policy</h1>
  <div class="meta">What RaidProtector reads, what it keeps, and for how long</div>
</div>

<div class="prose">
  <p class="updated">Last updated <%= updated %>. Operated by <%= operator %>.</p>

  <p>
    RaidProtector detects raids, which means that unlike its sibling BotApprove it does read
    message content. This page says exactly what it does with it. It is written against what the
    bot actually does rather than in general terms.
  </p>

  <h2>Message content</h2>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>RaidProtector reads messages in memory and does not write them down.</strong> It
      holds the Message Content intent because detecting a raid means noticing the same text
      posted by many accounts at once. Message text is used to compute a short fingerprint and is
      then discarded. The text itself is never written to the database and never logged.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is one exception, and it is off unless a server turns it on. A server may enable a
      <strong>second opinion</strong> on the rarest decisions, which sends recent messages from
      the affected channel to a language model run by a third party. It applies only where a ban
      was queued, never to ordinary detection, and it is capped per month. What the model says is
      recorded and acted on by nobody: the response is decided by the rules either way. Read
      <a href="#second-opinion">what that involves</a> before switching it on.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    What survives a message is a one-way hash of its content, the channel and author IDs, a
    timestamp, how many accounts or roles it mentioned, and two yes or no answers: whether it
    contained a Discord invite link, and whether it typed @everyone or @here. Those two are what
    the optional spam guard counts, and they are kept and discarded exactly like the hash. That
    is enough to tell that fifteen
    accounts posted the same thing in nine channels inside four seconds, which is what raid
    detection needs. It is not enough to reconstruct what was said, and neither the operator nor
    your moderators can read your messages through this bot.
  </p>
  <p>
    The practical consequence, stated plainly: when RaidProtector reports a raid, your moderators
    see the pattern, not a transcript. If you need the messages themselves, read them in Discord
    before they are deleted.
  </p>
  <p>Direct messages are not read at all. The bot only sees channels in servers it has been added to.</p>

  <h2>What is stored</h2>

  <h3>About servers</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Server ID and name.</li>
    <li>Your settings: thresholds, which response is enabled, exempt roles and channels, and the
      log channel.</li>
    <li>Licence state, which is shared with BotApprove under the Protection Suite licence.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>About people</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Discord user IDs</strong> of accounts involved in a detected raid, and of anyone
      who changes a setting or reverses an action.</li>
    <li>The username of an account named in a raid incident, so your moderators know who to look
      at.</li>
    <li>Account creation date and server join time, because the gap between them is one of the
      signals.</li>
    <li>Your Discord ID, username and avatar hash when you sign in to the dashboard, held in a
      server-side session.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      Some of this is about people who never chose to use this bot. If an account takes part in
      something RaidProtector flags, its ID is recorded in that server's audit trail. That is the
      point of the tool: a server owner needs to know who was involved.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3>About what happened</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Message fingerprints, channel IDs and timestamps, as described above. These are kept only
      as long as the detection window needs them and are then discarded.</li>
    <li>Join timestamps and counts, for detecting join floods.</li>
    <li>For each incident: when it started, which signal fired, how many accounts were involved,
      what response was configured, what was actually done, and whether it was reversed.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What is not stored</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Message content.</strong> Read in memory, fingerprinted, discarded. Never written
      to disk. If a server has switched on the second opinion described below, recent messages
      from the affected channel are sent to a third party at the moment a ban is queued, and are
      still not stored here.</li>
    <li><strong>Attachments, images, embeds or links</strong> are not downloaded or kept.</li>
    <li><strong>Direct messages.</strong> The bot does not read them.</li>
    <li><strong>Member lists</strong>, beyond the specific accounts named in an incident.</li>
    <li><strong>Voice data</strong>, presence, or activity.</li>
    <li><strong>Your Discord OAuth token.</strong> Used once at sign-in to read your identity and
      server list, then discarded. Never written to disk.</li>
    <li>Payment card details. Payment is handled by a payment provider, not by this service.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Verification</h3>
  <p>
    If a server switches on <code>/verify</code>, new members press a button to get a role. Nothing
    about who verified, when, or how is written down. The role is the only record, Discord holds
    it, and it goes when the person leaves the server.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Whether someone gets one press or the web step is decided at the moment they press, from
      what Discord already shows: account age, whether it has an avatar, Discord's own spam flag,
      and whether a raid is open in that server. None of it is saved.</li>
    <li>The web step is a page at <code>/verify</code> on this site. Your browser works out a
      number by trial. The page sets no cookie and creates no session. The code that says which
      server and which account the check is for sits after the # in the link, which browsers do
      not send to a server, so it is never in a request log. It reaches the server only inside
      the two requests the page makes, and is not kept after them.</li>
    <li>No IP address, browser fingerprint, email address or linked account is collected. Many
      verification bots collect these to catch alternate accounts. This one does not, by design.</li>
    <li>It never asks anyone to log in, scan a QR code, download anything or paste anything.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why it is stored</h2>
  <p>
    To detect raids, to give server owners an audit trail of what was done to their members and
    why, and to let an automated action be reviewed and reversed. Where a lawful basis is
    required, it is legitimate interest in protecting a Discord community from coordinated abuse,
    and contract performance for licensed features.
  </p>

  <h2 id="second-opinion">The second opinion, and what it sends</h2>
  <p>
    Off unless a server switches it on, and off for every server by default.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>When it runs.</strong> Only at the moment a ban is queued, which needs bans to be
      switched on, three separate signals, and an account with no tenure. It never runs on
      ordinary detection, on timeouts, or on an incident that was held.</li>
    <li><strong>What is sent.</strong> Recent messages from the channel the activity is loudest
      in, with the account names and ages already in the incident. Sent at that moment, read from
      Discord rather than from anything stored here.</li>
    <li><strong>Who receives it.</strong> OpenRouter, which passes it to the language model named
      in the operator's configuration. Their handling is governed by their own terms, not this
      policy.</li>
    <li><strong>What comes back.</strong> A verdict, a confidence and a short reason. It is
      recorded against the incident and shown in the report.</li>
    <li><strong>What it decides.</strong> Nothing. The response is decided by the rules whether
      the model agrees or not, and every report carrying a verdict says so on its face. It exists
      to build a record of whether the model is any good at this before anyone considers giving
      it a say.</li>
    <li><strong>How much of it.</strong> Capped per server per month, twenty calls by default.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you would rather none of this happened, leave it off, which it is. Nothing else in
    RaidProtector depends on it.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is shared with</h2>
  <p>
    Nobody. Data is not sold, rented, or given to advertisers or analytics services. There are no
    third-party trackers on this site. Incident records stay in the server they belong to and are
    not pooled across servers or published.
  </p>

  <h2>Where it lives</h2>
  <p>
    In a SQLite database on a private server, reachable only over an encrypted connection, running
    under an account with access to nothing else on the machine. Sessions are stored server-side
    so signing out actually invalidates them.
  </p>

  <h2>How long it is kept</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Message fingerprints and join counts:</strong> minutes. They exist only for the
      length of the detection window and are then dropped.</li>
    <li><strong>Settings:</strong> until you change them, or the owner runs
      <code>/uninstall</code>. Kept if the bot is removed, so re-adding it restores your
      configuration.</li>
    <li><strong>Incident records and the audit trail:</strong> kept, because that is what a server
      owner relies on after an attack, until <code>/uninstall</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Sessions:</strong> up to 7 days, or until you sign out.</li>
    <li><strong>Backups:</strong> one copy of the database a night, each kept for 14 days. Anything
      erased is gone from the backups once the last copy made before the erase is deleted, 14 days
      later at most. Message fingerprints are never in a backup, because they are never written to
      disk.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Deleted rows are overwritten in the database file, not just unlinked, so they cannot be
    recovered from it afterwards.
  </p>

  <h2>Automated decisions</h2>
  <p>
    This matters more here than for most bots, so it is spelled out. If a server enables it,
    RaidProtector can time out, kick or ban an account automatically, without a human looking
    first. That decision is made by software, from thresholds the server owner chose.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>During the beta the default is to report and not act. Acting has to be switched on.</li>
    <li>The server owner is never acted against, and neither is anyone whose top role is at or
      above the bot's.</li>
    <li>A circuit breaker stands the response down after several separate incidents in a short
      window. It counts incidents rather than actions, so one large raid does not switch it off
      partway through.</li>
    <li>Every action is logged with its reason and the signal that caused it, and the notification
      carries a way to reverse it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you were affected by an automated action and want it reviewed, contact the owner or
    moderators of that server first. They control the thresholds, they control the ban, and they
    can lift it. The operator of RaidProtector cannot undo a moderation action in someone else's
    server.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    You can see what is held about you, have it corrected, or have it erased. Depending on where
    you live you may also have the right to object to processing, or to complain to a data
    protection authority.
  </p>

  <h3>Erasing it yourself</h3>
  <p>
    You do not have to ask anyone. <code>/erase me</code> is for your own data, and
    <code>/uninstall</code> is for a whole server. Both work whether or not the server is in the
    beta, because erasing your data is never something a server has to pay for.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong><code>/erase me</code></strong> works in any server RaidProtector is in, and in a DM
      with the bot. It shows how many records name you and where, then asks before changing
      anything. Your ID is replaced with "erased" wherever it appears. The incident stays in the
      server's history without you in it, and anything RaidProtector holds about you in memory is
      dropped at the same moment. One consequence: an action taken on you during an incident can
      no longer be reversed from the bot, because it no longer knows who it was taken on. A
      moderator can still lift it by hand.</li>
    <li><strong><code>/uninstall</code></strong> is for the server owner, and only members with
      Administrator see it. It deletes every record about the server, settings, incidents and the
      learned baseline included, and clears what the bot holds in memory for it. RaidProtector then
      leaves, because staying would start a new record straight away. Nothing in the server itself
      is touched. It waits while an incident is open or bans are queued, because those records are
      what is needed to undo them. If the server still has a Protection Suite
      licence, its ID comes back on the next licence sync, because the licence lives in BotApprove;
      <code>/uninstall</code> there removes it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Both count again afterwards and show you how many records are left, which should be none. For
    a person, that count reads every column of every table in the database, not a list of the
    places the ID is expected to be.
  </p>
  <p>
    One wait, and the command explains it when it applies: if a ban on you is queued from an
    incident that is still in progress, <code>/erase me</code> holds until the ban lands or a
    moderator cancels it. Erasing first would aim the ban at nobody, and the command is not a way
    out of one. It is usually a few minutes.
  </p>
  <p>
    For anything the command does not cover, such as a correction, use the contact link below.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>
    The service is not directed at anyone below Discord's minimum age for their country. If you
    believe a child's data is held here, get in touch and it will be removed.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    This policy may change, and during the beta it is more likely to than most. Material changes
    will be noted by the date at the top of this page. If the bot ever begins storing message
    content, that would be a material change and it would be announced before it happened, not
    after.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Privacy requests go through
    <a href="<%= contactUrl %>" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">GitHub</a>.
  </p>

  <p class="updated">
    See also the <a href="/raidprotector/terms">RaidProtector Terms of Service</a>.
  </p>
</div>

